<Project>

  <!--
    Packed as build/MartenStudio.props, and it replaces the one the Static Web Assets SDK would have
    generated. That is the SDK's own supported extension point: see the comment above
    `GenerateStaticWebAssetsPackFiles` in Microsoft.NET.Sdk.StaticWebAssets.Pack.targets - "Authors
    including custom MSBuild targets into their packages are expected to disable the generation of
    $(PackageId).props files and to manually import build\Microsoft.AspNetCore.StaticWebAssets.props in
    their custom props files." The two imports at the bottom are exactly what the generated file
    contained; everything the package's static web assets do still happens.

    build/ rather than buildTransitive/ because the SDK still generates the other two entry points and
    both chain here: buildTransitive/MartenStudio.props imports buildMultiTargeting/MartenStudio.props
    imports this file. Whichever one NuGet picks for a given consumer, the property below is set.

    WHY THE PROPERTY IS HERE AT ALL. As of .NET 10, `_framework/blazor.web.js` is no longer embedded in
    the ASP.NET Core assemblies: it lives in the Microsoft.AspNetCore.App.Internal.Assets pack, and the
    SDK resolves that pack only for a project whose `RequiresAspNetWebAssets` is true. The Web SDK
    defaults it to true only when the consuming project has `.razor` Content items of its own
    (`ResolveRequiredWebAssets` in Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web.ProjectSystem.targets). A host that adds Marten
    Studio and no Blazor components of its own therefore has none: `GET /_framework/blazor.web.js`
    answers 404, the studio prerenders, it never becomes interactive, and nothing in the build says a
    word about it. Silent, and the studio looks like it works.

    So the package answers the question on the consumer's behalf. This has to be a .props rather than a
    .targets: NuGet imports .props before the project body and its SDK targets, which is where the
    conditional default is decided, and the `== ''` guard means anything the consumer says - in the
    project file, or in a Directory.Build.props - still wins.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RequiresAspNetWebAssets Condition="'$(RequiresAspNetWebAssets)' == ''">true</RequiresAspNetWebAssets>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Import Project="Microsoft.AspNetCore.StaticWebAssetEndpoints.props" />
  <Import Project="Microsoft.AspNetCore.StaticWebAssets.props" />

</Project>
